### How do orElse and orElseGet differ?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Optional;

public class OrElseVsOrElseGet {
    static String expensiveDefault() {
        System.out.println("Computing expensive default...");
        return "DEFAULT_ACCOUNT";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Optional<String> present = Optional.of("SAV2001");

        // orElse: expensiveDefault() CALLED even when present!
        String r1 = present.orElse(expensiveDefault());
        System.out.println("orElse result: " + r1);

        System.out.println("---");

        // orElseGet: expensiveDefault() NOT called when present!
        String r2 = present.orElseGet(OrElseVsOrElseGet::expensiveDefault);
        System.out.println("orElseGet result: " + r2);
    }
}
```

```text
Computing expensive default...
orElse result: SAV2001
---
orElseGet result: SAV2001
```

orElse called the expensive method even though the Optional had a value. orElseGet did not.

### When does the difference matter?

| Default cost | orElse | orElseGet | Recommendation |
|-------------|--------|-----------|----------------|
| Cheap constant | Trivial waste | Same result | Either is fine |
| Database query | Wasted query | No query | orElseGet |
| HTTP call | Wasted network | No call | orElseGet |
| Object creation | Wasted allocation | No allocation | orElseGet |

### Why is orElse eager?

orElse accepts a value, not a Supplier. Java evaluates method arguments before passing them. So `orElse(expensiveMethod())` calls expensiveMethod() first, then passes the result to orElse.

```java
// orElse(T defaultValue) – T is already computed
String r = opt.orElse("literal"); // cheap, fine
String r = opt.orElse(repository.findById("X")); // DB query runs regardless!

// orElseGet(Supplier<T>) – Supplier defers computation
String r = opt.orElseGet(() -> repository.findById("X")); // query only if empty
```

### What about orElseThrow?

orElseThrow follows the same lazy pattern: the exception Supplier is invoked only when the Optional is empty.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

BigDecimal balance = findBalance("SAV2001")
    .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException("SAV2001"));
// Exception created ONLY if empty. No waste.

// Java 10+: no-arg orElseThrow throws NoSuchElementException
BigDecimal balance = findBalance("SAV2001").orElseThrow();
```

### What is a real production scenario?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Optional;

public class AccountService {
    private final AccountRepository repository;
    private final AccountCache cache;

    public Account findAccount(String id) {
        // WRONG: orElse queries DB even on cache hit
        // return cache.get(id).orElse(repository.findById(id));

        // CORRECT: orElseGet queries DB only on cache miss
        return cache.get(id).orElseGet(() -> repository.findById(id));
        // With 80% cache hit rate: eliminates 80% of DB queries
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using orElse with side effects
Account a = findAccount(id).orElse(createDefaultAccount()); 
// createDefaultAccount() runs AND may insert into DB even when account exists!

// CORRECT: orElseGet defers the side effect
Account a = findAccount(id).orElseGet(() -> createDefaultAccount());
// Default created ONLY when account not found

// WRONG: orElse with expensive computation in a loop
for (String id : accountIds) {
    String name = cache.get(id).orElse(repository.findName(id)); // N DB queries
}

// CORRECT: orElseGet
for (String id : accountIds) {
    String name = cache.get(id).orElseGet(() -> repository.findName(id)); // only on miss
}
```

### What about or() (Java 9)?

```java
// or() returns an Optional, enabling chained fallbacks
Optional<Account> account = findInCache(id)
    .or(() -> findInLocalDB(id))
    .or(() -> findInRemoteService(id));
// Tries each source lazily until one returns a present Optional
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The rule: if the default involves any computation, I/O, or side effect, always use orElseGet.
- Under production load with 80% cache hit rate, orElseGet eliminates hundreds of wasted queries per second.
- In code reviews, flag any `orElse(repository.find(...))` as a performance bug.
- or() (Java 9) chains Optional sources lazily, useful for cache-then-DB-then-remote patterns.
